@@s033 Amortised cost
@@s034 An <b>amortised</b> cost is a bound on the <i>sum</i> of the costs of a sequence of operations, divided by the number of operations. A single operation can cost a lot, but the sequence does not. Example: the binary counter. Each increment changes many bits, but in 8 increments the total changes are 15: about 2 per operation, therefore <code>O(1)</code> amortised.
@@s035 The binary counter: how many bits change
